From f858d208e8482aa22afd43417fcde7c72a19c248 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Rafael=20Mendon=C3=A7a=20Fran=C3=A7a?=
 <rafael.franca@shopify.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 07:36:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ZJIT: Send Symbol block arguments directly

A send with a Symbol block argument such as `foo(&:sym)` stayed a
dynamic send: "block argument is not nil" for an ISEQ callee, "complex
argument passing" for a C function. Compile it to SendDirect for ISEQ
callees and to CCallWithFrame or CCallVariadic for C functions, with
the Symbol as the callee frame's block handler.

The block argument must be a static Symbol, known either from its type
(a literal) or from the profile with a GuardType. The interpreter uses
the Symbol as the block handler when Symbol#to_proc is basic in the box
of the running frame and the caller's cref has no refinements. The send
is specialized only while Symbol#to_proc is not redefined and no box has
been created with Ruby::Box.new, with patch points for both, and a guard
loads the cref and exits when it has refinements. `using` can add
refinements to a cref after the code is compiled, so this is checked on
every call rather than at compile time. Dynamic Symbols and other block
argument objects are handled as before.

Defining Symbol#to_proc again with its own definition clears the basic
flag without invalidating the method entry, so compiled code keeps
passing the Symbol. That gives the same result as calling
Symbol#to_proc, except under refinements, where the guard exits.

The callee does not change: its `yield` to a Symbol block handler is
still a generic invokeblock.

This is the Symbol part of Rafael's zjit-perf/blockarg-direct-send
branch in Shopify/ruby (3f17d8950e), narrowed and ported to current
master. That branch also sends forwarded block parameters directly and
passes Proc and nil block arguments to C functions; those parts are left
out here.

Co-authored-by: Thomas Klemm <github@tklemm.eu>
